    "created_at": "2022-02-28T14:18:00Z",
    "updated_at": "2022-02-28T14:20:08Z",
    "body": "> I think overall this sounds reasonable. I think the tricky bit is figuring out the policy container to be used for any fetches resulting from the 103. How will we deal with CSP, referrer, sandboxing, etc.\r\n> \r\n> Another challenge here is that currently Fetch is handed a \"client\" which doesn't really work for this. (Arguably that setup needs some changes anyway as Fetch needs to pull of what it needs before it goes in parallel and I'm not sure it does for everything.)\r\n\r\nConceptually, I suggest that a 103 would be somewhat equivalent to a `prefetch` by a document from the same origin as the navigation (which is also [yet to be fully specified](https://github.com/w3c/resource-hints/issues/86). \r\n\r\nSomething like:\r\n- A 103 response arrives\r\n- For the first 103, create a fake prefetching document with the URL of the response\r\n- Assign to that document the CSP known so far, e.g. CSP received by this or other 103 responses\r\n- Once the real document is created and initialized, apply the headers again as if they were regular [link headers](https://github.com/whatwg/html/pull/7622).\r\n\r\n(... not sure if the spec needs to involve this fake document in practice, but I'm suggesting it should behave in an equivalent manner)\r\n",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
